---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import DevEncodeBinary from '@/components/tools/text/DevEncodeBinary.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="binary-to-text">
  <DevEncodeBinary client:load kind="text" direction="decode" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>Decoding binary</h2>
    <p>
      Paste the ones and zeros. The tool discards all other characters (spaces, commas, newlines, stray
      letters) before decoding, so the formatting of the source does not matter. It splits the
      remaining bits into groups of eight (or seven in ASCII mode) and reads each group as a character
      code.
    </p>

    <h2>Do it by hand</h2>
    <p>Take <code>01010111</code>:</p>
    <pre><code>Place values: 128  64  32  16   8   4   2   1
Bits:           0   1   0   1   0   1   1   1
Contributing:      64      16       4   2   1
Total: 64 + 16 + 4 + 2 + 1 = 87  →  "W"</code></pre>
    <p>
      A shortcut for the common case: a byte that starts <code>010</code> is an uppercase letter, and a
      byte that starts <code>011</code> is lowercase or a digit. The last five bits give the position in
      the alphabet.
    </p>

    <h2>When the bit count does not divide evenly</h2>
    <p>
      In a valid binary message, the total bit count is a multiple of the group size. If there is a
      remainder, one of these is true:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>A digit was lost in the copy. Check the start and end of the paste.</li>
      <li>The source used 7-bit groups and you decode as 8, or the reverse.</li>
      <li>A 0 or 1 in the input was not data: a bullet point, a list number, a line label.</li>
      <li>The message was never binary text.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The error message gives the exact number of leftover bits, which usually shows the cause.</p>

    <h2>UTF-8 or single-byte?</h2>
    <p>
      If the decoded text has many <code>Ã</code>, <code>â</code> and <code>€</code> characters, you
      are reading UTF-8 bytes one at a time. Switch the character set to UTF-8 and the accented
      characters appear. If you get replacement diamonds in UTF-8 mode, the source was probably 7-bit
      ASCII or Latin-1, so switch the other way.
    </p>

    <h2>Common patterns you can recognise</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Byte</th><th>Character</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>00100000</code></td><td>space</td><td>The most common byte in English text</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>00001010</code></td><td>line feed</td><td>End of a line on Unix</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>00001101</code></td><td>carriage return</td><td>Paired with the above on Windows</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>00110000</code>–<code>00111001</code></td><td>digits 0–9</td><td>All start <code>0011</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>00000000</code></td><td>null</td><td>Padding, or a string terminator</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Where binary messages come from</h2>
    <p>
      Puzzle hunts, CTF challenges, escape rooms, geocaching clues, homework, and some technical cases:
      a logic-analyser capture, a serial trace, or an error message that printed a byte in binary. The
      tool reads the bits the same way in all cases.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
